In `tests/netpbm.hpp`, inside `read()`, strip the `\r` before the comment/empty checks:

```cpp
    // Helper function to read a line, skipping comments and empty lines
    auto readLine = [&file]() {
      std::string line;
      while (std::getline(file, line)) {
        if (!line.empty() && line.back() == '\r') {
          line.pop_back();
        }
        if (!line.empty() && line[0] != '#') {
          return line;
        }
      }
      return std::string{};
    };
```

Why this exact placement:

- `std::getline` on a CRLF file leaves the `\r` at the end of `line`, so `"P2\r"` becomes `"P2"` and the magic-number checks pass.
- Stripping *before* the comment check means `"#comment\r"` is still recognized as a comment and a lone `"\r"` line still counts as empty.
- It doesn't disturb the binary P6 path: `getline` already consumed the `\n`, so after popping `\r` the stream is positioned at the first pixel byte, and `file.read(...)` still reads the right bytes.
- `line.back()` is safe because of the `!line.empty()` guard.

That's a 3-line addition with no other edits. Say the word and I'll apply, commit, and push.

Here's the whitespace-tolerant version of the same helper:

```cpp
    // Helper function to read a line, skipping blank lines and comments
    auto readLine = [&file]() {
      std::string line;
      while (std::getline(file, line)) {
        const auto first = line.find_first_not_of(" \t\r\n");
        if (first == std::string::npos) {   // blank (or whitespace-only) line
          continue;
        }
        if (line[first] == '#') {           // comment, allowing leading whitespace
          continue;
        }
        const auto last = line.find_last_not_of(" \t\r\n");
        return line.substr(first, last - first + 1);
      }
      return std::string{};
    };
```

What it does:

- `find_first_not_of(" \t\r\n")` finds the first real character. If there is none, the line is blank and is skipped — including lines that are only spaces, tabs, or a stray `\r` from CRLF checkouts.
- A `#` as the first non-whitespace character is treated as a comment, so comments with leading indent are now handled too.
- `find_last_not_of(...)` trims trailing whitespace, including the `\r`, so the returned string is clean enough for the `"P1"`/`"P2"`/`"P3"`/`"P6"` checks. No separate `pop_back()` is needed.

This is safe for the binary P6 path: `readLine()` is only called for the three header lines (magic, dimensions, maxval), so skipping blank lines can't consume pixel bytes. After the last header line, `file.read(...)` starts at the first pixel byte as before.

